Minimum Civilizational Function Theory

A Viability-Based Generative Framework for the Structure of Extraterrestrial Professions

ouadi Maakoul


AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek,Meta AI

Abstract

We present a formal theory in which the professional structure required for a self‑sustaining extraterrestrial settlement emerges from first principles. The environment determines a viability‑constrained set of necessary functions; a minimal functional inventory—the Minimum Civilizational Function Set (MCFS)—is derived by explicitly coupling atomic functions to the state dynamics via capability operators. We distinguish between functions, professions, and human workforce, proving that increasing maturity can lead to more functions while demanding fewer human roles. A trust‑based multi‑agent allocation heuristic is shown to produce a candidate viable policy; global viability must be verified through a coupled resource‑precedence feasibility check. Redundancy‑aware profession generation is formulated as a set multi‑cover problem, extended with a multi‑mode beta‑factor reliability model and a diversity vector. Dependency criticality is approximated by a resource‑unconstrained critical path with a resource bottleneck penalty. Resilience is decomposed into topological (k‑connectivity via Menger’s theorem) and probabilistic (common‑cause and diversity) layers. Workforce evolution is modelled as a relaxation process; if the relaxation operator is contractive under graph‑edit distance, the profession ecosystem converges to a unique fixed point. A self‑improving genetic algorithm optimizes non‑safety parameters while keeping safety thresholds invariant; positive reachability of an optimal genotype and elitism guarantee almost‑sure asymptotic convergence. The framework’s causal responsiveness is measured by a sensitivity ratio that contrasts environmental displacement with algorithmic noise, and a suite of validation tests is proposed. The complete pipeline constitutes a generative theory that derives organizational structure from physical constraints, rather than cataloguing preconceived job titles.

---

1. Introduction

Planning a self‑sustaining extraterrestrial settlement typically begins with a list of professions: medic, engineer, botanist, technician. This approach embeds terrestrial occupational assumptions into a context where the physical environment is radically different. We argue that the professional structure of a civilization should be a consequence of the environment’s demands, not an import from Earth. The present work formalises this principle as a mathematical chain:

\text{Environment} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Viability Constraints} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Necessary Functions} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Agents \& Capabilities} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Professions \& Organisation}.

The theory rests on viability theory (Aubin, 1991): a settlement survives only if its state trajectory remains forever within a prescribed constraint set. Every viable control policy requires a collection of atomic functions—indivisible capabilities such as oxygen generation, radiation shielding, or medical intervention. These functions are the building blocks of professions. By grounding the entire derivation in the viability kernel, we ensure that the resulting professional ecosystem is causally linked to survival, not to cultural precedent.

Previous work on crew selection and habitat design (e.g., NASA’s Human Research Program) largely assumes a fixed set of job categories. Multi‑agent task allocation (Gerkey & Matarić, 2004) and set‑cover formulations have been used to assign tasks, but they do not generate the tasks themselves. Our contribution is a fully generative pipeline: from raw environmental parameters to a resilient profession graph, with formal guarantees on termination, completeness, and convergence under specified conditions.

The paper is structured as follows. Section 2 embeds atomic functions into the state dynamics and defines the Minimum Civilizational Function Set (MCFS) from the viability kernel. Section 3 distinguishes functions, professions, and human workforce, showing how automation can decouple their growth. Section 4 introduces the concept of necessary functions and an essentiality measure. Section 5 treats trust‑based multi‑agent allocation with coupled constraints. Section 6 formulates profession generation as a redundancy‑aware set cover with a multi‑mode beta‑factor reliability model. Section 7 separates topological and probabilistic resilience. Section 8 models dynamic workforce relaxation under a conditional contraction. Section 9 presents the self‑improving genetic algorithm with safety‑hard parameters. Section 10 proposes a rigorous causal sensitivity metric and a validation programme. Sections 11 and 12 discuss limitations and conclude.

