2. Viability and the Functional Dynamics

We model the settlement state as a vector x(t) \in X \subset \mathbb{R}^n capturing resource stocks, infrastructure integrity, crew health, and knowledge. The state space X is compact. The dynamics are given by

x(t+1) = F_0\!\big(x(t), u(t)\big),

where u(t)\in U(x) is a control, and F_0 describes the intrinsic physical processes (e.g., resource depletion, decay, natural radiation) in the absence of any engineered function. A hard viability constraint set D \subset X\times U encodes all survival limits (oxygen partial pressure, radiation dose, water‑loop closure, energy margin, etc.). The viability kernel \mathrm{Viab}(F_0, D) is the set of initial states from which there exists a control sequence keeping the trajectory in D forever. Under standard compactness and semicontinuity assumptions, the kernel can be approximated by the Saint‑Pierre algorithm.

2.1 Capability Operators

A function f is not merely a label; it modifies the state dynamics. We model a function as a capability operator

\Gamma_f : X \times U_f \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n,

which adds a deterministic increment to the state when the function is active. For example, the function “oxygen generation” contributes a term \Gamma_{\text{O2}}(x, u_{\text{O2}}) that raises the oxygen stock depending on power and feedstock. The joint effect of a set of functions \mathcal{F} is

F_{\mathcal{F}}(x,u) = F_0(x,u_0) + \sum_{f\in\mathcal{F}} \Gamma_f(x,u_f) + \sum_{\substack{f_i,f_j\in\mathcal{F}\\ i<j}} \Gamma_{ij}(x,u_{ij}),

where the quadratic terms capture nonlinear interactions (e.g., waste heat from power generation affecting thermal control). For tractability, we often assume additive superposition or restrict to a linear combination of basis operators. The control space decomposes as U_{\mathcal{F}}(x) = U_0(x) \times \prod_{f\in\mathcal{F}} U_f(x).

2.2 Minimum Civilizational Function Set

Definition 1 (MCFS). Given an environment E (physical parameters, hazards, resource availability) and a maturity level L with associated viability constraints D_L, the Minimum Civilizational Function Set is

\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L) = \arg\min_{\mathcal{F}\subseteq \mathcal{F}_{\text{univ}}} |\mathcal{F}|
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}}, D_L) \neq \emptyset,

where \mathcal{F}_{\text{univ}} is a finite universe of candidate atomic functions. The viability kernel now depends on the dynamics F_{\mathcal{F}} induced by the function set.

Theorem 1 (Monotonicity of MCFS). For increasing maturity L, |\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L+1)| \ge |\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L)|.

Proof. Higher maturity imposes stricter constraints, D_{L+1}\subseteq D_L. Any \mathcal{F} viable for D_{L+1} is also viable for D_L. The collection of feasible function sets shrinks, so its minimum cardinality cannot decrease. ∎

The connection to concrete dynamics via \Gamma_f makes MCFS a genuine viability‑derived object rather than a separate set‑cover construction.

