3. Distinguishing Functions, Professions, and Human Workforce

The MCFS counts atomic functions, not human job titles. A single human may perform several functions, and many functions can be automated. We therefore introduce three separate objects:

· \mathrm{MCFS}(E,L) – the minimum set of functions required for viability.
· \mathrm{MCPS}(E,L) – the minimum number of professions (bundles of functions assigned to one human or robotic role) that cover all necessary functions with the required redundancy.
· \mathrm{MCHW}(E,L) – the minimum number of human agents needed when the professions are allocated among humans, robots, AI, and hybrids.

Theorem 2 (Complexity–Occupational Decoupling). As maturity increases, the number of necessary functions is non‑decreasing (|\mathrm{MCFS}_{L+1}| \ge |\mathrm{MCFS}_L|), while the number of human professions may decrease (|\mathrm{MCHW}_{L+1}| \le |\mathrm{MCHW}_L|) if automation substitutes for human labour.

Proof. The first part is Theorem 1. The second part follows from the feasibility of allocating functions to non‑human agents. As technology improves, the trust in automated agents rises, allowing the same set of functions to be covered by fewer human‑staffed professions. Hence the minimum cardinality of a human‑staffed cover can shrink even though the function set expands. ∎

This decoupling is a crucial theoretical result: civilisation can become more functionally complex while requiring fewer distinct human occupational roles.

