4. Necessary Functions and Essentiality

Not all functions in an MCFS are equally critical. A function is civilizationally necessary if its removal destroys viability.

Definition 2. A function f\in F is necessary for viability under environment E and maturity L if

\mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}}, D_L) \neq \emptyset \quad\text{and}\quad \mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}\setminus\{f\}}, D_L) = \emptyset.

The set of all such functions is denoted N(E,L). Every N(E,L) is a subset of every MCFS, giving a core of indispensable capabilities.

Definition 3 (Essentiality Index). Let V(\mathcal{F}) be a measure of the viability kernel’s “size” (e.g., its Lebesgue measure in a reduced space). The essentiality of f is

\mathrm{Ess}(f) = 1 - \frac{V(\mathcal{F}\setminus\{f\})}{V(\mathcal{F})},

with V(\emptyset)=0. A necessary function has \mathrm{Ess}(f)=1; a wholly redundant function has \mathrm{Ess}(f)=0.

This index provides a continuous spectrum of criticality, directly grounded in viability.

