6. Profession Generation as Redundancy‑Aware Set Cover

A profession is a bundle of functions that can be performed by a single human (or robot role). Candidate professions are formed by clustering functions based on shared competency requirements and responsibility levels. The profession generation problem is to select a minimal set of professions covering all functions, with additional redundancy for critical functions.

6.1 Multi‑Mode Beta‑Factor Reliability

Redundant professions share infrastructure and are subject to common‑cause failures (CCF). Instead of a single \beta, we index failure modes: habitat loss, power system failure, software fault, training deficiency, etc. Let p_k be the failure probability of a profession due to mode k, and \beta_k the fraction of such failures that are common‑cause. The probability that all r redundant professions fail simultaneously is

P_{\text{all fail}} = 1 - \prod_{k} \bigl(1 - \beta_k p_k - (1-\beta_k) p_k^{\,r}\bigr).

Given a tolerable risk \varepsilon, the required redundancy r_{\min} is the smallest integer satisfying P_{\text{all fail}} \le \varepsilon. We further demand a diversity condition: at least two distinct infrastructure groups must be represented among the providers of each critical function, so that a single common‑cause event does not eliminate all paths.

6.2 Set Multi‑Cover Formulation

Let F be the universe of functions, with F_{\text{crit}} the critical subset. The coverage requirement is \mathrm{req}(f) = r_{\min} for f\in F_{\text{crit}} and 1 otherwise. Candidate professions are subsets S \subseteq F. The problem is to find a minimum‑size subcollection P such that every f is covered at least \mathrm{req}(f) times. This is a set multi‑cover problem, NP‑hard by reduction from minimum set cover.

We employ a greedy algorithm: iteratively pick the candidate covering the largest number of unmet needs (unit weight per still‑needed coverage), update residual requirements, and repeat. Standard analyses give an approximation ratio of O(\log |F|). Ties are broken using a composite score derived from requirement priorities, betweenness centrality, and automation feasibility, normalised to a fixed‑anchor scale. A lexicographic filter retains human‑supervisory professions for functions with criticality above a fixed safety threshold, even if automation is technically feasible.

