7. Resilience: Topological and Probabilistic Layers

Resilience of the profession ecosystem is analysed in two layers.

7.1 Topological Resilience

We extend the profession DAG by adding supply nodes for each profession and sink nodes for critical functions. An edge exists from a profession to a function if the profession provides that function. Menger’s theorem (node version) states that the maximum number of internally node‑disjoint paths from source professions to a critical function equals the minimum size of a node cut. If there are at least k disjoint paths, then any failure of k-1 professions (nodes) cannot disconnect the function from all supply. We call this k‑topological resilience.

7.2 Probabilistic Resilience

Topological redundancy alone does not guarantee reliability if the remaining paths share a hidden common cause. The beta‑factor diversity condition (distinct infrastructure groups, software independence, etc.) ensures that no single event can simultaneously compromise all disjoint paths. We formally define a Civilizational Diversity Vector for each critical function: D_f = (d_{\text{power}}, d_{\text{habitat}}, d_{\text{software}}, d_{\text{training}}, \dots), where each component is 1 if at least two distinct providers of that function differ in that dimension. Probabilistic resilience requires D_f \ge D_{\min} (componentwise) and the multi‑mode beta‑factor bound P_{\text{all fail}} \le \varepsilon.

Theorem 3 (Joint Resilience). If a critical function has k-topological resilience (k \ge 2) and satisfies the probabilistic resilience conditions, then the reliability target \varepsilon is met against both independent and common‑cause failures up to k-1 profession losses.

Proof. The topological condition ensures that no set of k-1 profession failures eliminates all supply paths. The probabilistic condition bounds the failure probability of the remaining diverse paths. The product of the individual bounds (or the joint beta‑factor formula) yields the overall survival probability. ∎

