8. Dynamic Ecosystem Relaxation

Between major technological jumps, the profession ecosystem adjusts through incremental reorganisation (merging redundant roles, removing obsolete ones). We model the state as a labelled graph P representing professions and their dependencies. The relaxation operator \Phi_{\text{relax}} applies a set of cost‑reducing transformations.

Assumption 1 (Conditional Contraction). Under a weighted graph‑edit distance d with positive edit costs, the operator \Phi_{\text{relax}} is a contraction: there exists \alpha < 1 such that d(\Phi(P_1),\Phi(P_2)) \le \alpha\, d(P_1,P_2) for all P_1,P_2.

Theorem 4 (Conditional Convergence). If Assumption 1 holds and the metric space of profession graphs (bounded by a maximum size) is complete, then the iteration P_{n+1} = \Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_n) converges to a unique fixed point P^* at a geometric rate.

Proof. The Banach fixed‑point theorem applies under the contraction condition. The finite state space is complete because every Cauchy sequence is eventually constant; with a genuine contraction, the distance to the fixed point shrinks strictly until it reaches zero after a finite number of steps. ∎

The contraction property must be empirically verified for a given relaxation operator; it is a modelling hypothesis, not an unconditional guarantee. If the operator is merely non‑expanding, convergence to a local optimum can still be shown via a Lyapunov argument.

