9. Self‑Improving Civilizational DNA

The “Civilizational DNA” encodes all tunable parameters: decomposition rule preferences, cost weights, pairwise comparison matrices (quantised), and efficiency thresholds. Safety‑critical parameters—\theta_{\text{auto}} (maximum automation feasibility for human removal), \theta_{\text{crit}} (criticality threshold for mandatory human oversight), and the beta‑factor‑derived r_{\min}—are held constant and not subject to evolution.

The DNA is represented as a fixed‑length binary string, yielding a finite search space. A genetic algorithm (GA) with elitism and a mutation operator (per‑bit flip with small reset probability) evolves the population. The fitness of a DNA configuration is evaluated by running the full pipeline on a reference environment and aggregating scores over multiple criteria (coverage, redundancy, cost, resilience). The weights of these criteria are fixed and not evolvable.

Theorem 5 (Almost‑Sure Convergence). If there is a positive probability of reaching a globally optimal genotype from any population, and elitism preserves the best individual, then the maximum fitness in the population converges to the global optimum with probability 1 as the number of generations tends to infinity.

Proof sketch. The transition kernel on the population space is such that from any non‑optimal population, there is a strictly positive probability of generating an optimal string (via mutation) in the next generation. Elitism ensures that once an optimal string appears, the best fitness never declines. Standard convergence results for elitist evolutionary algorithms on finite spaces (Rudolph, 1994) then imply almost‑sure convergence. ∎

