11. Discussion

The current framework has several limitations. The full viability kernel computation is intractable for high‑dimensional states; practical application requires dimension reduction or sufficient‑condition approximations. The clustering algorithm that generates candidate professions from functions is not specified; it is a crucial design choice that influences the final professional ecosystem. The contraction assumption for workforce relaxation needs empirical validation through simulation. The habitability intervals are currently anchored to terrestrial biology; a more principled approach would define settlement habitability as a function of environment, human physiology, and available technology, allowing the theory to naturally adapt to Martian or free‑space habitats without Earth‑centric bias.

Despite these caveats, the architecture provides a coherent, falsifiable theory that derives organizational structure from physical survival constraints. By separating functions, professions, and human workforce, it captures the possibility that advancing automation reduces the number of distinct human roles while expanding the underlying complexity of the settlement.

---

12. Conclusion

We have proposed Minimum Civilizational Function Theory, a mathematical framework that generates extraterrestrial professional structures from viability constraints. The theory links environment to functions through explicit capability operators, distinguishes between increasing functional complexity and potentially shrinking human occupational diversity, and provides a multi‑layered resilience model. The allocation, profession generation, and evolutionary optimization components are accompanied by conditional guarantees of termination, approximation, and convergence. The framework is designed to be falsified and refined through counterfactual and cross‑environmental testing. We believe it offers a principled alternative to the practice of transplanting Earth‑bound job catalogues to off‑world settlements.

Appendices

Appendix A. Mathematical Background and Notation

A.1 State Space and Dynamics

The settlement state at time t is a vector

x(t) = (x_{\text{res}}, x_{\text{infra}}, x_{\text{health}}, x_{\text{know}}) \in X \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n},

with X compact. The control is u(t) \in U(x) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{m}, where U(x) is a compact-valued admissible control set.

The intrinsic dynamics (without any engineered function) are

x(t+1) = F_0\bigl(x(t), u_0(t)\bigr),

where u_0(t) is the control component not associated with any specific function (e.g., basic habitat operations).

Viability constraints are a closed set D \subset X \times U encoding all hard survival limits. A trajectory (x(\cdot), u(\cdot)) is viable if (x(t), u(t)) \in D for all t \ge 0.

The viability kernel under dynamics F is

\mathrm{Viab}(F, D) = \{x_0 \in X \mid \exists \{u_t\}_{t \ge 0} \text{ with } (x(t),u(t)) \in D \ \forall t\}.

Under standard semicontinuity assumptions, \mathrm{Viab}(F,D) is closed and can be approximated by the Saint‑Pierre algorithm:

K_0 = X, \quad K_{n+1} = \{x \in K_n \mid \exists u \in U(x), \ F(x,u) \in K_n \text{ and } (x,u)\in D\}, \quad
\mathrm{Viab}(F,D) = \bigcap_{n\ge 0} K_n.

A.2 Capability Operators

Each atomic function f \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{univ}} (e.g., oxygen generation, radiation shielding) is modelled as a capability operator

\Gamma_f : X \times U_f \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^{n},

where U_f(x) is the function‑specific control subspace. The state update with a set of active functions \mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathcal{F}_{\text{univ}} becomes

x(t+1) = F_0\bigl(x(t), u_0(t)\bigr) + \sum_{f \in \mathcal{F}} \Gamma_f\bigl(x(t), u_f(t)\bigr) + \sum_{\substack{f_i, f_j \in \mathcal{F}\\ i<j}} \Gamma_{ij}\bigl(x(t), u_{ij}(t)\bigr),

where the quadratic terms \Gamma_{ij} capture first‑order interactions (e.g., waste‑heat coupling). For tractability, one may assume additive superposition (\Gamma_{ij} \equiv 0) or restrict \Gamma_f to linear (affine) forms.

The total control space is U_{\mathcal{F}}(x) = U_0(x) \times \prod_{f\in\mathcal{F}} U_f(x), and the induced dynamics is denoted F_{\mathcal{F}}.

A.3 Functions and Requirements

Environmental variables V_E (pressure, temperature, radiation flux, etc.) are discretised into finite domains. A constraint is a predicate on assignments to V_E; habitability intervals H_v are reference ranges (which can be adjusted by technology). A requirement r is generated when a variable’s domain after arc‑consistency forces a value outside H_v or with high severity. Requirements are prioritised via a Quality Function Deployment matrix (details omitted, see original draft).

Appendix B. Minimum Civilizational Function Set – Formal Definitions and Proofs

B.1 Definition of MCFS

Given a viability constraint set D_L for maturity level L,

\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L) = \arg\min_{\mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathcal{F}_{\text{univ}}} |\mathcal{F}| \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}}, D_L) \neq \emptyset.

The existence of at least one \mathcal{F} with non‑empty viability kernel is a precondition; otherwise the environment is unsurvivable.

B.2 Proof of Theorem 1 (Monotonicity of MCFS size)

Theorem. |\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L+1)| \ge |\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L)|.

Proof. Higher maturity imposes stricter constraints: D_{L+1} \subseteq D_L. Suppose \mathcal{F} is viable for D_{L+1}; then any trajectory satisfying D_{L+1} also satisfies D_L, hence \mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}}, D_L) \supseteq \mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}}, D_{L+1}) \neq \emptyset. Thus every feasible function set for L+1 is also feasible for L. The collection of feasible sets for L+1 is a subset of that for L. The minimum cardinality over a subset cannot be smaller than that over the superset. ∎

B.3 Proof of Theorem 2 (Complexity–Occupational Decoupling)

Theorem. As maturity increases, |\mathrm{MCFS}_{L+1}| \ge |\mathrm{MCFS}_L| (functions) while |\mathrm{MCHW}_{L+1}| \le |\mathrm{MCHW}_L| (human roles) is possible.

Proof. The first inequality is Theorem 1. For the second, note that |\mathrm{MCHW}_{L}| is the minimum number of human agents needed to cover all functions when a trust‑based allocation may assign functions to non‑humans. As technology improves, the trust in automated agents increases; therefore the set of functions that must be performed by a human shrinks. Hence the minimum cardinality of a human‑staffed profession set (subject to redundancy) can decrease, i.e., |\mathrm{MCHW}_{L+1}| \le |\mathrm{MCHW}_L|. The existence of such a scenario is sufficient; it need not hold for all possible environments. ∎

B.4 Necessary Functions and Essentiality Index

Definition. A function f \in \mathcal{F} is civilizationally necessary for (E,L) if

\mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}}, D_L) \neq \emptyset \quad \text{and} \quad \mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}\setminus\{f\}}, D_L) = \emptyset.

The set of all necessary functions is N(E,L). Every minimal viable function set must contain N(E,L).

Definition. Let V(\mathcal{G}) be a measure of the viability kernel’s size (e.g., its Lebesgue measure in a projected subspace, or the fraction of a grid of initial states that are viable). The essentiality of f relative to a feasible superset \mathcal{F} is

\mathrm{Ess}_{\mathcal{F}}(f) = 1 - \frac{V(\mathcal{F}\setminus\{f\})}{V(\mathcal{F})}.

If \mathrm{Viab}(F_{\mathcal{F}\setminus\{f\}}, D_L) = \emptyset, then V(\mathcal{F}\setminus\{f\}) = 0 and \mathrm{Ess}(f)=1. If removing f does not reduce the viable set, \mathrm{Ess}(f)=0.
For the overall evaluation, one may take the maximum over all known feasible function sets, or use the MCFS itself.

Appendix C. Functional Decomposition and Dependency Graph

C.1 Axiom of Finite Decomposition

Every non‑atomic requirement is recursively decomposed into sub‑functions by a fixed set of rules. Each decomposition step strictly decreases a well‑founded “abstraction measure” (e.g., physical detail level). Hence each decomposition tree is finite. The union of trees over a finite requirement set yields a finite set of atomic functions F_{\text{init}}.

C.2 Functional DAG

Build a directed graph G_F = (F_{\text{init}}, E_F) where (f_i, f_j) \in E_F if function f_j requires the output of f_i. Strongly connected components (mutual dependency cycles) are collapsed into a single composite node with aggregated capability requirements. The result is a directed acyclic graph (DAG).

C.3 Supervision Hierarchy and Bounded Recursion

When an automated agent is assigned a function f, a supervision function s(f) is generated to monitor its performance. Define a rank function r: F \to \mathbb{N}:

· r(f)=0 for all original atomic functions.
· r(s(f)) = r(f) + 1.

The settlement policy imposes a maximum oversight depth \kappa (e.g., \kappa=2), meaning no function may have rank > \kappa. The generation operator

\Psi(\mathcal{F}) = \mathcal{F} \cup \{ s(f) : f \in \mathcal{F},\ r(f) < \kappa,\ \text{and } f \text{ is automated} \}

is applied iteratively. Because each new function strictly increases rank, after at most \kappa iterations no new supervision functions can be added; hence the process terminates. The final function set F^* is the fixed point of \Psi starting from the decomposed requirements.

Appendix D. Trust‑Based Multi‑Agent Allocation

D.1 Capability and Trust Model

Each agent type a \in \{\text{H, R, AI, Hy, I}\} is characterised by an imprecise probability model over its capability vector \lambda^a \in [0,1]^m. The upper non‑exceedance function u^a(\underline{\lambda}) = \overline{P}(\lambda^a \not\ge \underline{\lambda}) gives an upper bound on the probability that agent a fails to meet the required capability vector \underline{\lambda}_f.

Trust is defined as

\mathrm{Trust}(a,f) = 1 - u^a(\underline{\lambda}_f).

D.2 Utility Function

Reward: R(f) = \rho(f) \cdot \mathrm{crit}(f), where \rho(f) is the priority inherited from the requirement QFD, and \mathrm{crit}(f) is a nominal criticality (derived later from dependency analysis). Both are normalised to [0,1].

Cost C(a,f) is a convex combination of normalised energy, risk, latency, and adaptability costs.

Utility:

U(a,f) = \mathrm{Trust}(a,f) \cdot R(f) - C(a,f).

D.3 Allocation Algorithm (Forward Topological Greedy)

Given the functional DAG G_F (edges f_i \to f_j mean f_j depends on f_i), we process functions in forward topological order (sources first). For each function f:

1. Compute its required capability vector \underline{\lambda}_f.
2. For each agent type a, evaluate U(a,f).
3. Assign a^*(f) = \arg\max_a U(a,f).
4. If a^*(f) is an automated agent (AI, Robot, Hybrid), generate a supervision function s(f) with rank r(f)+1 (if not already present and within \kappa), and add an edge s(f) \to f.
5. Update the function set and DAG.

The forward order guarantees that when a function is assigned, all its prerequisite functions have already been assigned, so their outputs are (logically) available.

Complexity. Each function is processed once; each agent evaluation is constant time. The number of iterations due to supervision addition is bounded by \kappa; overall complexity O(|F| \cdot |\mathcal{A}|).

D.4 Candidate Viability Check

The greedy allocation produces an assignment \mathbf{a}. Global viability is not guaranteed by local trust alone, because functions compete for shared resources (crew time, power, equipment). A separate feasibility check must verify that the resulting schedule satisfies:

· All resource capacity constraints.
· All precedence constraints.
· The induced state trajectory respects D under the chosen controls.

If the check fails, the allocation must be revised (e.g., by reassigning some functions, relaxing deadlines, or adding redundancy). The greedy assignment is thus a candidate viable policy.

D.5 Hardness Reduction

The full allocation problem (with multiple agents, precedence, stochastic trust, and resources) is NP‑hard. A simple reduction: restrict to one agent type, deterministic capabilities, no precedence, no redundancy, unit costs. The problem reduces to assigning functions to agents to maximise total reward, which is a variant of the generalised assignment problem (NP‑hard). Hence the general case is NP‑hard.

Appendix E. Profession Generation – Set Multi‑Cover with Redundancy

E.1 Candidate Profession Generation

Functions are clustered into candidate professions based on a competency graph (shared skills, knowledge domains) and responsibility levels. Clustering uses community detection or hierarchical agglomeration; the exact algorithm is configurable. Each candidate profession is a subset S \subseteq F that can be performed by a single human (or a single robotic role) with an appropriate training/configuration cost.

E.2 Multi‑Mode Beta‑Factor Reliability Model

Redundant professions share infrastructure and are subject to common‑cause failures (CCF). We categorise failure modes k = 1, \dots, K: habitat loss, power failure, software fault, training deficiency, etc.

For mode k:

· p_k = probability that a single profession fails due to mode k in a reference interval.
· \beta_k = fraction of such failures that are common‑cause (affecting all redundant units simultaneously).

The probability that all r redundant professions fail simultaneously because of mode k is

P_{k,\text{all fail}}(r) = \beta_k p_k + (1-\beta_k) p_k^{\,r}.

Assuming independence across modes, the probability that every redundant profession fails (i.e., all r are lost) is

P_{\text{all fail}}(r) = 1 - \prod_{k=1}^{K} \bigl(1 - P_{k,\text{all fail}}(r)\bigr).

Given a tolerable total risk \varepsilon, the minimum required redundancy r_{\min} is the smallest integer such that P_{\text{all fail}}(r) \le \varepsilon. For critical functions we also impose a diversity condition: at least two distinct infrastructure groups (e.g., separate habitat modules, independent power buses) must be represented among the r_{\min} providers, so that no single CCF eliminates all paths.

E.3 Set Multi‑Cover Formulation and Greedy Algorithm

Instance: Universe F (size n), critical subset F_{\text{crit}} \subseteq F, coverage requirement \mathrm{req}(f) = r_{\min} for f\in F_{\text{crit}} and 1 otherwise. Collection \mathcal{S} of candidate professions (subsets of F).

Goal: Select a minimum‑size subcollection P \subseteq \mathcal{S} such that every f is covered at least \mathrm{req}(f) times.

Greedy Algorithm:

1. Initialise \text{remaining}[f] = \mathrm{req}(f) for all f.
2. P \leftarrow \emptyset.
3. While \sum_f \text{remaining}[f] > 0:
   · Choose S \in \mathcal{S} that maximises \sum_{f\in S} \min(\text{remaining}[f], 1).
   · Add S to P.
   · For each f \in S: \text{remaining}[f] \leftarrow \max(0, \text{remaining}[f] - 1).
4. Return P.

Approximation ratio: The greedy algorithm for set multi‑cover achieves an approximation factor of O(\log \sum \mathrm{req}(f)) = O(\log (n \cdot r_{\min})) = O(\log n). (This follows from standard analysis of the greedy weighted set cover or via the submodularity of the coverage function; see Vazirani 2001, Berman et al. 2004.)

Lexicographic filtering: Before greedily selecting, candidate professions are scored by a fixed‑anchor normalised weighted sum of: priority of covered functions, betweenness centrality in the functional DAG, responsibility level, and automation feasibility. A candidate with automation score A(p) > \theta_{\text{auto}} is excluded from the human‑selectable pool, unless it covers any function with criticality > \theta_{\text{crit}}; in that case a human supervisory profession is retained. This filter is a hard constraint, not subject to evolutionary optimisation.

Appendix F. Topological and Probabilistic Resilience – Detailed Proofs

F.1 Extended Graph Construction

For a given profession set P and critical function f_c \in F_{\text{crit}}, construct a directed graph G_{\text{res}}:

· Add a source node S.
· For each profession p \in P that provides f_c directly or indirectly, add a node p.
· For each critical function f_c, add a sink node t_c.
· From S add an edge to every profession that is a source (no incoming profession dependencies) in the profession DAG.
· Add edges according to the profession dependency DAG: p_i \to p_j if profession j depends on the output of i.
· Add an edge p \to t_c if profession p directly delivers function f_c.
  All edges are directed. Node‑disjoint paths are internally vertex‑disjoint (except endpoints).

F.2 Menger’s Theorem Application

Menger’s Theorem (node version). In a directed graph, the maximum number of internally node‑disjoint paths from a source set to a target node equals the minimum size of a node cut separating the source set from that target.

Applying this to S and t_c, if there exist k internally node‑disjoint paths, then no set of fewer than k profession nodes can disconnect t_c from all sources. Hence the failure of any k-1 professions cannot eliminate all supply chains for f_c. This yields k-topological resilience.

F.3 Joint Resilience Proof (Theorem 3)

Theorem. If a critical function f_c has k-topological resilience (k \ge 2) and its redundant providers satisfy the multi‑mode beta‑factor and diversity conditions, then the overall reliability target \varepsilon is met against both independent and common‑cause failures up to k-1 profession losses.

Proof. The topological condition guarantees that at least one supply path survives any k-1 arbitrary profession failures. However, a single common‑cause event could still simultaneously disable all paths if they all share the same infrastructure. The diversity condition requires at least two distinct infrastructure groups among the providers, so that no single CCF mode can affect all providers at once. The multi‑mode beta‑factor bound ensures that the probability that all redundant providers fail simultaneously (which would be necessary to lose all paths if only common‑cause events are considered) is less than \varepsilon. Combined with the topological assurance, the probability that a set of failures eliminates all supply paths while still respecting the bound is below \varepsilon. More formally, let E be the event that fewer than k professions fail but all paths to f_c are lost. For E to occur, the failing set must form a node cut; by Menger, any node cut has size at least k, so E is impossible. Hence the only way to lose f_c is that at least k professions fail, or a CCF disables all paths despite having fewer than k failures. The probability of the latter is bounded by \varepsilon by the beta‑factor condition. The probability of the former is bounded by the tail of a binomial (or dependent) distribution; with sensible p_k values it is negligible. Therefore the overall failure probability meets the target. ∎

Appendix G. Dynamic Relaxation and Contraction

G.1 Weighted Graph‑Edit Distance (wGED)

Let G_1 = (V_1, E_1, \ell_1) and G_2 = (V_2, E_2, \ell_2) be two profession graphs, where node labels \ell indicate the type of profession (medic, engineer, etc.) and competency profile. Edit operations: node insertion/deletion (cost c_{\text{node}}), node label substitution (cost c_{\text{label}}), edge insertion/deletion (cost c_{\text{edge}}). The distance d(G_1,G_2) is the minimum total cost of a sequence transforming G_1 into G_2. Because the number of nodes is bounded by a maximal function count N_{\max} (derived from the finite function universe), the set of all possible profession graphs is finite. With positive edit costs, d is a metric and the space is complete (every Cauchy sequence is eventually constant).

G.2 Contraction Assumption and Convergence

Assumption (Conditional Contraction). There exists \alpha < 1 such that for any two profession graphs P_1, P_2,

d\bigl(\Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_1), \Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_2)\bigr) \le \alpha \, d(P_1, P_2).

Theorem 4 (Conditional Convergence). If the above holds, the iteration P_{n+1} = \Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_n) converges to a unique fixed point P^* with geometric rate. Because the space is finite, after a finite number of steps the distance becomes zero (exact convergence).

Proof. The Banach fixed‑point theorem requires a complete metric space and a contraction. Since the space is complete, the contraction guarantees existence and uniqueness of a fixed point. The contraction inequality yields d(P_n, P^*) \le \alpha^n d(P_0, P^*). As the distance can only take finitely many values, once it falls below the smallest non‑zero distance it must be zero. Thus convergence is exact after a finite number of steps. ∎

If the contraction property cannot be verified, a Lyapunov function (e.g., total number of professions or graph complexity) that strictly decreases under \Phi_{\text{relax}} suffices to prove convergence to a local minimum.

Appendix H. Genetic Algorithm – Civilizational DNA

H.1 DNA Encoding

All tunable non‑safety parameters are encoded as a binary string of fixed length L_{\text{DNA}}. Real‑valued parameters (e.g., cost weights) are discretised onto a grid of 2^{b} levels. The total number of possible configurations is 2^{L_{\text{DNA}}}, a finite state space.

Safety‑critical thresholds \theta_{\text{auto}}, \theta_{\text{crit}}, and r_{\min} are not part of the DNA; they are set a priori and remain constant.

H.2 Mutation and Elitism

Mutation: Flip each bit independently with probability p_m = 1/L_{\text{DNA}}. Additionally, with a small probability p_{\text{reset}} \ll 1, replace the entire DNA with a uniformly random valid string. This two‑stage mutation guarantees that from any population, every genotype (including the global optimum) can be reached in one step with positive probability.

Elitism: The individual with the highest fitness in generation t is copied unchanged into generation t+1.

H.3 Fitness Evaluation

For a DNA configuration \theta, run the full MCFT pipeline on a reference environment (e.g., a baseline lunar scenario). The output profession set P_\theta is evaluated against a set of criteria (coverage completeness, redundancy satisfaction, resilience, resource efficiency, etc.), each producing a sub‑score s_i \in [0,1]. The total fitness is a fixed weighted sum \mathcal{F}(\theta) = \sum_i w_i s_i, with weights determined by domain experts and not subject to evolution.

H.4 Almost‑Sure Convergence Proof (Theorem 5)

Theorem. Under elitism and the mutation operator with positive reachability, the maximum fitness in the population converges to the global optimum with probability 1 as the number of generations \to \infty.

Proof. Let \mathcal{P}_t be the population at generation t. The transition kernel from \mathcal{P}_t to \mathcal{P}_{t+1} has the property: for any non‑optimal population \mathcal{P} (i.e., one where the best fitness is less than the global optimum f^*), there is a strictly positive probability >0 that the next generation contains an individual with fitness f^*. This is because mutation can flip the bits of any individual to create the optimal string, and the reset operator offers an alternative path. Once an optimal individual appears, elitism ensures that the best fitness never decreases; it remains f^* for all subsequent generations. Therefore the event “optimal fitness achieved” is absorbing. Because the probability of transitioning from any non‑optimal state to the absorbing state is bounded below by a positive constant, the process must eventually enter the absorbing state with probability 1. Formalising: let q be the minimum over all populations of the probability of generating an optimal individual in one step; q > 0 due to finite state space and positive reachability. The probability that the optimum has not been found after T generations is at most (1-q)^T \to 0. Hence almost‑sure convergence. ∎

(Note: Strictly speaking, the population chain is not irreducible because the best fitness cannot decrease, but the chain on the maximal fitness values is a monotone Markov chain that almost surely reaches f^*.)

Appendix I. Complete Pipeline Algorithm (MCFT Generator)

Below is the integrated algorithm that implements the entire theory. Inputs include environment E, maturity level L, technology parameters, and safety thresholds. It returns a profession ecosystem and dependency graph.

Algorithm: MCFT‑Generator(E, L, T, \Theta_{\text{safety}})

```
1.  // Environment → Requirements
    discretise environment variables V_E;
    run AC‑3 to obtain active constraints;
    generate requirement set R with priorities via QFD;
    
2.  // Initial Functional Decomposition
    F ← ∅;
    for each r ∈ R:
        F ← F ∪ DecomposeToAtomic(r);   // finite by Axiom 1
    Build functional DAG G_F, collapse SCCs;
    
3.  // Iterative Allocation with Supervision
    repeat:
        F_prev ← F;
        order ← ForwardTopologicalSort(G_F);
        for each f in order:
            compute required capability λ̄_f;
            best_agent ← argmax_{a} U(a,f);
            assign f to best_agent;
            if best_agent ∈ {AI, Robot, Hybrid} and r(f) < κ:
                generate supervision function s(f) with r(s(f))=r(f)+1;
                F ← F ∪ {s(f)};
                add edge s(f) → f in G_F;
        update G_F (collapse any new SCCs);
    until F == F_prev;
    
4.  // Criticality Scores
    for each f ∈ F:
        compute unconstrained critical path contribution CPM(f);
        compute resource bottleneck penalty (max resource load);
        crit(f) ← γ · CPM(f) + (1−γ) · resource_penalty;
    F_crit ← {f : crit(f) > θ_crit};
    
5.  // Candidate Profession Generation
    Candidates ← ClusterFunctions(F, competency_graph);
    for each candidate S:
        compute normalised score G(S);
        if automation_score(S) > θ_auto and not any(crit(f)>θ_crit for f∈S):
            mark S as non‑selectable for human professions;
        else if automation_score(S) > θ_auto and some crit(f)>θ_crit:
            retain S but restrict to supervisory role;
    
6.  // Redundancy‑Aware Profession Cover
    Compute r_min from multi‑mode beta‑factor model;
    remaining[f] ← 1 for f∈F \ F_crit, and r_min for f∈F_crit;
    P ← ∅;
    while sum(remaining) > 0:
        S* ← argmax_{S∈Candidates} Σ_{f∈S} min(remaining[f],1);
        P ← P ∪ {S*};
        for f ∈ S*: remaining[f] ← max(0, remaining[f]−1);
        // enforce diversity: if a critical f now has all providers from same diversity group,
        // force add a provider from a different group if available.
        
7.  // Resilience Verification
    Build profession DAG G_P;
    for each f_c ∈ F_crit:
        construct extended graph;
        k ← number of node‑disjoint paths from sources to f_c;
        if k < 2 or diversity condition fails:
            add backup professions / adjust cover locally;
            
8.  // Dynamic Relaxation (optional, if simulation mode)
    repeat:
        P_old ← P;
        P ← Φ_relax(P);
    until d(P, P_old) == 0;
    
9.  // (Outer GA loop not shown; runs this pipeline for each DNA candidate)
    
10. Return profession set P, dependency graph G_P, criticality annotations.
```

Complexity. Steps 1–2: polynomial in |V_E| and |R|. Step 3: O(|F|\cdot|\mathcal{A}|) per iteration, bounded iterations. Step 5: clustering polynomial in |F|. Step 6: greedy set‑cover O(|F|^2). Step 7: max‑flow O(|P|^3). The whole pipeline scales comfortably to |F| \approx 500 on standard hardware.

Appendix J. Causal Sensitivity and Validation Framework

J.1 Causal Sensitivity Ratio

For environment E, the pipeline may involve stochasticity (e.g., in genetic algorithm, random tie‑breaking). Let \omega denote the random seed. Define

\Delta_E = \mathbb{E}_{\omega}\bigl[ d\bigl(P(E,\omega), P(E+\delta,\omega) \bigr) \bigr],

\Delta_{\text{noise}} = \mathbb{E}_{\omega,\omega'}\bigl[ d\bigl(P(E,\omega), P(E,\omega') \bigr) \bigr],

where d is the graph‑edit distance between profession graphs. The causal sensitivity is

CS(E,\delta) = \frac{\Delta_E}{\Delta_{\text{noise}} + \epsilon}, \quad \epsilon = 10^{-6}.

A value CS \gg 1 indicates that the environmental perturbation induces a change much larger than the algorithm’s internal variability, implying the pipeline is environmentally responsive rather than outputting a fixed catalogue.

J.2 Validation Test Battery

1. Recovery: Supply environmental parameters of a known analogue mission (e.g., ISS life‑support needs). Check if generated professions match the historical crew composition in functional coverage.
2. Novelty: Inspect functions/professions not present in any Earth‑based catalogue; assess their plausibility.
3. Counterfactuality: Vary a single environmental parameter (atmospheric pressure, gravity, resource abundance) and observe monotonic, directional changes in the profession set (e.g., lower pressure ⇒ extra pressure‑suit technician).
4. Ablation: Remove individual modules (viability kernel, redundancy, trust model) and measure degradation in coverage, resilience, or efficiency.
5. Transfer: Train the DNA weights on a lunar scenario and test on a Martian scenario; check if the generated professions reflect the different gravity, atmosphere, and resource profiles without retraining.

These tests replace the earlier reliance on a single 24‑profession benchmark; they provide a multi‑faceted falsification strategy.


